## Objective
Replace broad experimentation with the smallest defensible comparison set capable of supporting or falsifying the central maintained NeuroCAD claim.

## Required matrix
Document and, where feasible, implement:
- naive baseline;
- strongest relevant classical baseline;
- relevant neural baseline;
- strongest easy competitive baseline;
- ablation removing the claimed NeuroCAD mechanism;
- at least one negative/control condition.

For every row specify dataset, seed policy, primary metric, secondary metric, statistical comparison, expected runtime, and failure/falsification criterion.

## Definition of done
The experiment matrix can be executed from documented commands and would allow a skeptical reviewer to determine whether the maintained claim survives reasonable baselines.

When `v0.5.0a6` is cut, retain provenance from the actual tag-triggered release artifacts rather than copying local candidate values.

Required evidence:
- [ ] exact tag → commit mapping;
- [ ] GitHub Actions release workflow URL / run ID;
- [ ] Python / platform matrix and browser acceptance state used by the release workflow;
- [ ] published wheel and normalized sdist names + SHA-256 values;
- [ ] generated `SHA256SUMS`;
- [ ] generated `RELEASE_PROVENANCE.json` and its binding to the tagged commit;
- [ ] installer command used in the clean credential-free verification;
- [ ] verification environment and timestamp;
- [ ] installed `neurocad --version` / `doctor` identity;
- [ ] bounded generation + compiled/verified artifact receipt from the installed release;
- [ ] confirmation that the GitHub release is marked prerelease.

Do not mark complete before the tagged release exists and these values are observed from the real published artifacts. Release provenance is engineering/reproducibility evidence and does not change scientific claim status.

## Objective
Run the first scientifically admissible matched direct-vs-structured pilot without altering the frozen methodology after outcome access.

## Before any provider call
Freeze and review:
- exact benchmark/pilot selection + hashes;
- provider/model/revision;
- decoding parameters;
- prompt bundle receipt/hash;
- seed set;
- identical retry budget and feedback policy for both arms;
- exact call and cost ceilings;
- OpenSCAD/verifier/environment identity;
- paired primary endpoint and McNemar/bootstrap analysis plan;
- `authorized=true` only after the above are reviewed.

## Execution requirements
- Retain every raw response and failed attempt.
- Preserve hash-chain ledger and artifact hashes.
- No post-outcome prompt/schema/baseline/seed/metric edits.
- Distinguish infrastructure failures from scientific failures.
- Report direct HVR, structured HVR, paired delta, exact McNemar result, bootstrap CI, failure taxonomy, retries, latency/tokens/cost.

## Claim boundary
The historical typed-parser mechanism remains falsified. Stage 1 scripted fixtures remain engineering evidence only. A Stage-2 pilot is exploratory until the frozen primary protocol and stated inference boundary justify otherwise.

## Objective
Consolidate the active NeuroCAD product-pilot/commercial-readiness work that historically lived in `vertex-studyAI/vertexED.ai` into the canonical NeuroCAD repository.

## Historical source trackers
- vertex-studyAI/vertexED.ai#458 — external engineering pilot tracker
- vertex-studyAI/vertexED.ai#486 — pilot evidence schema
- vertex-studyAI/vertexED.ai#488 — commercial GREEN definition
- vertex-studyAI/vertexED.ai#470 — convert outreach into verified design partners

Those issues remain historical evidence; this issue is the active owner going forward.

## Pilot evidence contract
For every external test retain, where permission allows:
- organization/reviewer role;
- date;
- exact bounded workflow/input;
- expected behavior;
- observed behavior;
- pass/fail/ambiguous;
- artifact or screenshot reference;
- failure mode and severity;
- reproducibility;
- requested edit/regeneration;
- retest outcome;
- permission boundary for public attribution.

## Progression gates
- [ ] first external engineer completes one bounded workflow;
- [ ] first externally discovered reproducible failure is retained;
- [ ] first external failure is fixed and retested;
- [ ] 3 independent engineering teams complete evidence-backed tests;
- [ ] at least 2 repeat workflow tests are completed;
- [ ] one bounded organization pilot is completed;
- [ ] findings are summarized without promoting outreach/replies into validation.

## Commercial-readiness boundary
`COMMERCIAL_GREEN` requires a canonical release identity, clean current CI/release evidence, reproducible bounded CAD generation/edit/regeneration, a maintained pilot kit, >=3 evidence-backed design partners, >=2 repeat tests, and one bounded organization pilot. Until those gates exist, use a narrower state such as `DESIGN_PARTNER_READY` or `BLOCKED_EXTERNAL`.

Do not claim arbitrary natural-language-to-CAD, manufacturing validity, STEP/B-rep support, CFD/FEA/propulsion performance, airworthiness/certification, or broad external validation from limited pilot evidence.

## Routing
All new NeuroCAD product/pilot/commercial work belongs in `THE-BU1LD/NeuroCAD`. VertexED may retain historical links only.

## Objective
Preserve and route the fabrication-aware NeuroCAD successor concept out of VertexED without mutating the already-started current S3 protocol.

Historical source: `vertex-studyAI/vertexED.ai#621`.

## Ordering rule
The current S3 freeze/execution lane remains owned by `THE-BU1LD/NeuroCAD#36`. Do not silently retrofit fabrication/FEA/physical-test criteria into an already-frozen or partially frozen S3 after outcome access. Treat this issue as a separately versioned successor unless #36 is explicitly still pre-outcome and a documented protocol-version decision is made before evaluation access.

## Candidate axes
Report separately rather than collapsing into one score:
1. representation/CAD validity;
2. functional-geometry preservation;
3. structural viability under a predeclared material/load case where applicable;
4. fabrication-process validity under frozen printer/process assumptions;
5. sim-to-real/physical agreement on a small preregistered subset.

## Baselines
- reference/original CAD where available;
- current NeuroCAD generator without fabrication-aware selection;
- simple rule/repair baseline where applicable;
- any simulation-guided successor only after its protocol is separately frozen.

## Pilot gate
- [ ] freeze 10–20 tasks spanning >=3 failure modes;
- [ ] freeze prompts/models/seeds and process/material/load assumptions before outcomes;
- [ ] retain every generated artifact and failure;
- [ ] freeze object-level analysis and advancement criterion;
- [ ] no cherry-picking successful prints or load cases.

## Claim boundary
Any positive result applies only to the tested tasks, process/material assumptions and load cases. It does not establish general manufacturability, universal structural validity, or revive the historical typed-parser mechanism.

## Objective
Finish the migration/provenance handoff from the historical embedded NeuroCAD surfaces in `vertex-studyAI/vertexED.ai` into this canonical repository without rewriting evidence.

Historical source tracker: `vertex-studyAI/vertexED.ai#491`.

## Required map
Enumerate and cross-link the historical NeuroCAD surfaces that remain relevant to provenance:
- runtime/product implementation paths;
- e2e/browser tests;
- scripts and workflows;
- public-alpha/release documentation;
- research protocols/results/claim ledgers;
- Project 2424 NeuroCAD evidence/aliases;
- external pilot kit and evidence templates.

For each migrated or retained historical surface record:
- original repository path;
- source commit/blob identity where recoverable;
- canonical destination or explicit `HISTORICAL_ONLY` status;
- whether content is identical, migrated with non-scientific edits, or intentionally not migrated;
- cross-link needed for future reviewers.

## Integrity boundary
Do not recreate missing evidence, rewrite frozen results, or treat migration completeness as scientific validation. Preserve the typed-parser falsification and current Stage-2/S3 claim boundaries.

## Green gate
A reviewer can trace every material NeuroCAD product/research evidence surface from the historical VertexED location to its current canonical owner or an explicit historical-only record.

## Objective
Protect canonical `main` without weakening NeuroCAD's maintained engineering CI or conflating merge authorization with scientific authorization.

## Current verified state — 15 September 2026
- canonical `main` is `a910f949694787da012fe10818cf5367b51ac0f4`;
- direct branch readback reports `protected=false` with required status-check enforcement off;
- current-main restack PR #57 is being used to verify the stale #52 reproducibility/control-plane delta against the actual current parent;
- scientific outcome access, external challenge execution, and VeriCodeGen/S3 outcome access remain separate human/protocol gates and must never be made implicit consequences of a merge.

## Acceptance gate
1. require pull requests before merge;
2. require stable maintained NeuroCAD CI contexts that run on every merge candidate;
3. require at least one independent approval where repository staffing supports it;
4. block force pushes and branch deletion;
5. preserve fail-closed security/type/test/package/browser checks rather than weakening them to satisfy protection;
6. keep research/outcome authorization separate from branch protection;
7. preserve an explicit audited emergency/admin bypass only if genuinely required;
8. re-read the branch/ruleset after configuration and record the exact enforced contexts.

## Boundary
No source, dependency, database, production, secret, scientific protocol, threshold, held-out outcome, research claim, or execution-authorization change is authorized by this issue. The connected GitHub engineering surface can verify the missing protection but does not expose repository-administration writes needed to enable it, so the final enforcement step remains an owner/admin action.

## External test case

Coreform pointed us to the public **Rimac Automobili GBX 700** assembly on GrabCAD as a useful dirty-CAD case: large overclosures plus watertightness problems in the two main housings can separate “looks fine” from “analysis/meshing ready.”

This issue turns that suggestion into a reproducible benchmark. It is not a claimed Cubit integration.

## Frozen first-pass procedure

1. Preserve an untouched **as-imported** copy.
2. Record:
   - Cubit version;
   - source file format;
   - import settings;
   - units;
   - source URL / retrieval identity.
3. Run `validate volume all verbose` and retain the complete output.
4. Run Geometry Power Tools checks for:
   - small curves;
   - small surfaces;
   - bad angles;
   - thresholds explicitly stated relative to intended mesh size.
5. For the assembly, separately record:
   - gaps;
   - overlaps / overclosures;
   - misalignments;
   - which contacts/clearances are intentional versus defects.
6. If bad ACIS geometry is flagged, heal **only on a copy**, retain the baseline, and rerun the same checks.
7. Imprint/merge interfaces only where shared mesh nodes are intentionally required; record that decision.
8. Run the tet-meshing attempt separately from geometry validation.

## Required evidence

- [ ] exact input identity / provenance;
- [ ] baseline import receipt;
- [ ] raw validation output;
- [ ] Geometry Power Tools thresholds + findings;
- [ ] repair/defeature log with before/after identities;
- [ ] tet-mesh command/config;
- [ ] mesh success/failure and diagnostics;
- [ ] one compact table distinguishing visual plausibility, geometry validity, and meshability;
- [ ] reproducible runbook another person can follow.

## Claim boundary

Passing geometry validation does **not** establish meshability, simulation correctness, or engineering suitability. A failed mesh is a valid benchmark outcome. Do not modify the baseline until it succeeds and do not claim Coreform endorsement.

Source guidance received from Coreform on 1 Oct 2026; use their public documentation/forum for product-specific questions.

## Execution infrastructure added

The NeurIPS/physical-world evidence branch now contains the capture machinery for this benchmark:

- `research/protocols/COREForm_DIAGNOSTIC_CAPTURE_2026-10-01.md` — retained Coreform-derived capture protocol;
- `scripts/validate_coreform_capture.py` — standard-library structural validator;
- `tests/test_coreform_capture.py` — provenance/hash/path/stage-consistency tests;
- `research/captures/rimac_gbx700/capture.template.json` — pre-execution manifest with all outcome stages `NOT_RUN`;
- `research/captures/rimac_gbx700/README.md` — exact execution order and publication boundaries.

The validator deliberately does **not** run Cubit or interpret scientific correctness. It only verifies capture integrity. This preserves the distinction in the protocol between a structurally valid evidence package and an actually successful geometry or mesh result.
